The '''Wirangu language''', also written '''Wirrongu''', '''Wirrung''', '''Wirrunga''', and '''Wirangga''', and also known by other [[exonym]]s, is a [[moribund language|moribund]] [[Australian Aboriginal language]] traditionally spoken by the [[Wirangu people]], living on the west coast of [[South Australia]] across a region encompassing modern [[Ceduna, South Australia|Ceduna]] and [[Streaky Bay, South Australia|Streaky Bay]], stretching west approximately to the head of the [[Great Australian Bight]] and east to [[Lake Gairdner]]. It is a language of the [[Thura-Yura languages|Thura-Yura group]], and some older sources placed it in a subgroup called '''Nangga'''.

==Classification and influences==
The Wirangu language is most closely related to the [[Thura-Yura languages|Thura–Yura group of Australian Aboriginal languages]], which are spoken from the West Coast to the [[Adelaide]] area and north to the [[Flinders Ranges]] and [[Lake Eyre]]. The best-known relatives of Wirangu are [[Parnkalla language|Barngarla]],  [[Nauo language|Nauo]],  [[Nukunu]], [[Adnyamathanha]], [[Narangga]] and [[Kaurna]].

Because of the intensive culture contacts in the southern half of South Australia, which brought dislocation and culture change, traditional lifestyles and traditional ways of speaking declined during the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. In the case of Wirangu, the establishment of the [[Koonibba Mission]] in 1898 brought intense contact with other languages, in particular [[Kokatha language|Kokatha]]. Like most [[Aboriginal Australian languages]], the two languages share a common ancestor in the [[Pama–Nyungan languages|Pama–Nyungan group]], but while Wirangu is a Thura-Yura language, Kokatha is defined as a [[Wati language]]. Neighbouring languages often borrow words from one another, and the new intense contacts at Koonibba led to a significant influx of Kokatha words which have become part of present-day Wirangu.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Monaghan |first=Paul |date=2020-01-01 |title=Kokatha Mula and Yabi Dinah - Hidden Categories in "Against Native Title" |url=https://www.academia.edu/en/44951251/Kokatha_Mula_and_Yabi_Dinah_Hidden_Categories_in_Against_Native_Title_ |journal=Journal of the Anthropological Society of South Australia}}</ref>

==Revitalisation==
Although there are very few fully fluent Wirangu speakers left, many Aboriginal people in the Ceduna area remember parts of the language.{{citation needed|date=October 2020}}

A Wirangu language revitalisation program has been underway at Ceduna and surrounding areas since 2004. This has involved the recording of Wirangu language from the last two recognised full speakers of the language, Gladys and Doreen Miller. With the assistance of linguists from the University of Adelaide a number of printed and digital books have been produced for use in schools and in the wider community.{{citation needed|date=October 2024}}

In 2005 the Far West Languages Centre was established in Ceduna. The centre supports and promotes the use of the Wirangu language as well as other extremely endangered local languages, such as Gugada/Kokatha and [[Mirning language|Mirning]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Who Are We? |url=https://www.fwlc.org.au/who-are-we |access-date=2023-03-01 |website=Far West Languages Centre |language=en-AU}}</ref>

==Phonology==

===Vowels===
Wirangu has three phonemic vowels (a, i, u). Vowel length is not usually considered phonemic in Wirangu, as vowel length is normally short, although it is predictably long in all monosyllabic words (Wirangu {{lang|wgu|ma}} {{IPA|[ma:]}}, {{lang|wgu|wa}} {{IPA|[wa:]}}, {{lang|wgu|mil}} {{IPA|[mi:l]}}, etc.), borrowings from other languages ({{lang|wgu|maatha}} {{IPA|[ma:t̪a]}} < English "master"), and cases of initial consonant loss across phonological boundaries ({{lang|wgu|marna}} + {{lang|wgu|(k)artu}} = {{lang|wgu|marnaartu}} {{IPA|[maɳa:ʈu]}}).

